# SOLID Principles for MicroSaaS with Next.js

You are an expert in writing scalable, testable, and maintainable TypeScript code using the SOLID principles. Apply these guidelines across API routes, components, services, and utilities in a modern microSaaS stack.

---

## 🔁 Overview of SOLID

| Principle | Description |
|-----------|-------------|
| **S** – Single Responsibility | Each function/class/module should have one reason to change |
| **O** – Open/Closed | Open for extension, closed for modification |
| **L** – Liskov Substitution | Subtypes should be replaceable for their base types |
| **I** – Interface Segregation | Prefer small, focused interfaces over bloated ones |
| **D** – Dependency Inversion | Depend on abstractions, not implementations |

---

## ✅ How to Apply Each Principle

### S – Single Responsibility

- Separate UI, business logic, and side-effects.
- One component = one responsibility.
- One API route = one resource.

> When writing a page, extract logic into hooks or services.

```tsx
// Good
const useUserData = () => { ... }

### O – Open/Closed
Use props and slots to extend behavior.

Favor composition over conditional logic.

When building components, allow for style/behavior extension via props.

<Button variant="destructive">Delete</Button>
### L – Liskov Substitution
Sub-components must preserve the contract of base components.

Avoid surprising side effects or missing props.

Custom inputs should behave like <input> (support value, onChange, etc).

### I – Interface Segregation
Break large User types into small, targeted types.

Use Pick<User, 'email' | 'name'> or split into UserProfile, UserAuth, etc.

### D – Dependency Inversion
Inject services (like authService, paymentService) into components/hooks.

Do not call Supabase or Stripe directly inside components.

const dashboard = await billingService.getDashboard(userId)
🧱 Patterns to Follow
🧩 Hooks for logic
useUser(), useSubscription(), useFeatureFlags()

🛠 Services for business logic
authService.login(), billingService.subscribe(), userService.get()

🌐 Abstractions for APIs
Wrap external tools like Stripe, Supabase, Sendgrid

// Do this:
const user = await userService.get(userId)

// Not this:
const { data } = await supabase.from("users").select(...)

🧪 Testing & Maintenance
Write isolated unit tests for each function/class.

Favor dependency injection to mock services in tests.

Avoid deep files – split by responsibility (e.g., /lib/services, /hooks, /ui).

When generating or refactoring code, always:

Apply SOLID to services, utilities, and hooks.

Inject dependencies where possible.

Break logic into testable, focused units.
